$search_local_video — search FAISS indexes and return video fragments

SUMMARY
  Encodes each text prompt or videos[] query clip as a SigLIP embedding (same
  frame sampling as $video2embedding for video queries), searches files[] (.faiss
  indexes with .ahvmap.json mappings from $create_video_index), cuts the top
  matching fragments from source videos, and tags each result with label
  search_results. Multiple prompts[] or videos[] entries run separate searches;
  results are combined.

SYNTAX
  @hits: @index -> $search_local_video
  walking on the beach
  @hits: @index -> $search_local_video(n=5)

INPUT
  files[]  — videos.faiss (or videos.ahvmap.json in emulate) from $create_video_index
  prompts  — optional text search queries (instruction body or prompts[])
  videos[] — optional query clip(s); each is embedded like $video2embedding

OUTPUT
  videos[] — one MP4 fragment per hit (all queries combined)
  labels[] — search_results entries with query, start, end, closeness metadata

ARGUMENTS
  n=     — number of fragments to return per query (default 10)
  every= — sample every Nth frame for videos[] queries (default ~5 frames)
  model= — default | 224 | base-224 (same as $image2embedding)
  gpu=   — use CUDA when available

LABEL METADATA
  search_results third element:
    query     — prompt text or query video path that produced this hit
    src       — source video path
    ahvemb    — source .ahvemb path from index mapping
    start     — 0-based inclusive frame
    end       — 0-based exclusive frame
    closeness — inner-product similarity (higher = closer match)

DEPENDENCIES
  uv sync --extra media --extra video_index
  ffmpeg for fragment export (tools/download_ffmpeg.py)

ENVIRONMENT
  AH_EMULATE_SEARCH_LOCAL_VIDEO=1 — placeholder fragments, no model/ffmpeg.
  AH_SEARCH_LOCAL_VIDEO_GPU=0     — force CPU.

EXAMPLE
  @index: @all_videos -> $create_video_index
  @hits: @index -> $search_local_video
  sunset over water
  @similar: @index -> $file('query.mp4') -> $search_local_video(n=5)

PROMPT-CONSUMING
  Yes — clears prompts[] on output.
